<p>Platelet-rich plasma has become the default upsell in Indian hair clinics, which is unfortunate, because it is a reasonable treatment for a narrow group of people and largely wasted money for everyone else. The honest version is less exciting than the brochure: an adjunct, with evidence that is real but modest, that has to be repeated indefinitely.</p>

<h2 id="what-prp-is-and-what-it-is-meant-to-do">What PRP is, and what it is meant to do</h2>

<p>Your blood is drawn, spun in a centrifuge to separate the plasma fraction carrying a high concentration of platelets, and that concentrate is injected into the thinning scalp. Platelets carry growth factors, and the proposal is that delivering them around miniaturising follicles pushes those follicles back towards a longer growth phase, improving the calibre of hairs still present.</p>

<p>That is a mechanism, and a plausible one. The distance between a plausible mechanism and a proven clinical benefit is where most of the marketing lives. PRP does nothing to the androgen-driven process that drives <a href="/blog/norwood-scale-explained">pattern loss through the Norwood stages</a>, and it creates no follicles where the follicle has gone. At best it improves hair that is still growing, and it has to keep doing so to keep the effect.</p>

<h2 id="what-the-published-reviews-report">What the published reviews report</h2>

<p>Systematic reviews of PRP in androgenetic alopecia have been published repeatedly over the past decade, and the shape of their conclusions is consistent. Most report an improvement in hair density or hair count against baseline or a control injection, then flag substantial limitations in the trials they pooled: small numbers, short follow-up, and outcome measures that differ from paper to paper. The reviewers describe the evidence as promising rather than settled, and that wording is doing work.</p>

<p>Dermatology reference sources reflect the same caution. DermNet lists platelet-rich plasma among treatments used in <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/male-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">male pattern hair loss</a> alongside the medical therapies with the larger evidence base, not in place of them, and the same holds for <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/female-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">female pattern hair loss</a>.</p>

<h3>Why the protocols are so heterogeneous</h3>

<p>The reason those reviews hedge is that the trials are not comparing the same treatment. There is no standardised PRP. Between studies, and between clinics, all of the following vary:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The preparation system.</strong> Different kits and spin protocols yield different platelet concentrations, volumes and white-cell content.</li>
  <li><strong>Whether the platelets are activated</strong> before injection, and with what.</li>
  <li><strong>Injection depth, volume and spacing</strong> across the treated area.</li>
  <li><strong>The number of sessions and the interval between them.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>How the outcome was measured</strong> — a photographic impression, a global assessment score, or a machine count of density in a tattooed reference area. Those are not comparable.</li>
</ul>

<p>So when a clinic quotes a figure for how well PRP works, ask which protocol it came from and whether that is the protocol you are being sold. Usually the number has been borrowed, which is why <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-guarantees-and-success-rates">quoted success rates deserve scepticism</a> across this field.</p>

<h2 id="who-tends-to-respond">Who tends to respond, and who does not</h2>

<p>This is what decides whether the money is well spent, and it is settled at examination rather than by asking for the treatment by name.</p>

<h3>The group that tends to respond</h3>

<p>People with early to moderate pattern thinning, where the scalp under magnification still shows follicles present but miniaturised — hairs of visibly reduced calibre, thick and fine shafts mixed together. There is something there to improve. Trichoscopy establishes that, and doubles as the baseline against which later change is judged.</p>

<h3>The group that does not</h3>

<p>Scalp that has been bald for years. Where the follicle has been lost, no injection restores it and no growth factor recruits one that is not there. A smooth, shiny crown is not a PRP problem; it is a coverage problem, and the honest options are surgical or cosmetic. If that describes you, <a href="/blog/prp-or-hair-transplant">the comparison between PRP and a transplant</a> is the more useful read.</p>

<p>A third group is worth naming: people whose shedding is not pattern loss at all. Diffuse shedding after illness, a crash diet, childbirth or a thyroid problem is a different condition, and injecting it changes nothing about the trigger. Telling <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">reactive shedding apart from pattern hair loss</a> comes first, and in women it matters more still, because a treatable cause sits behind the loss far more often — see <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">how female pattern loss is assessed</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-sessions-are-usually-spaced">How sessions are usually spaced</h2>

<p>Described here as usual practice, not as a promise. Most protocols run an induction phase of roughly three to four sessions about four weeks apart, then a formal review, then maintenance every few months if there has been a response worth maintaining. The spacing is convention, not rule.</p>

<p>Two things follow. Nothing can be judged early: hair cycles slowly, a change in shaft calibre is not visible within a month, and a course sold on a visible difference after the first session is being sold on something other than biology. And the review point matters more than the sessions do — a course with no scheduled assessment built into it is a subscription rather than a treatment plan, which is one of several things <a href="/blog/what-to-ask-before-prp-in-gurgaon">worth asking before you pay for a course</a>.</p>

<h2 id="why-the-effect-recedes-if-you-stop">Why the effect recedes if you stop</h2>

<p>PRP does nothing to the underlying cause. In pattern hair loss the process is genetic and hormonal, runs continuously, and is not paused by an injection. What PRP appears to do, in the people it helps, is offset some of that ongoing loss for as long as it is delivered.</p>

<p>Stop, and the offset stops with it. The follicles resume miniaturising at whatever rate they were going to, and over the following months the gain recedes towards the trajectory you would have been on anyway. That is not the treatment failing; it is what maintenance treatments do, medical therapies for pattern loss included.</p>

<blockquote>The useful question is not whether PRP works. It is whether it works enough, for you, to be worth repeating indefinitely — and that is a question about your examination findings and your budget, not about the treatment in the abstract.</blockquote>

<p>Anyone presenting PRP as a one-off fix has either misunderstood it or is relying on you to. Ask what happens when you stop; the answer should be the paragraph above.</p>

<h2 id="prp-alongside-medical-therapy">PRP alongside medical therapy</h2>

<p>In pattern hair loss PRP is rarely the whole plan. The established medical treatments act on the mechanism actually causing the loss and carry a far larger evidence base; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS overview of hair loss</a> is a neutral summary of where they sit.</p>

<p>PRP is therefore usually an addition for someone already on appropriate medical therapy, or for someone who cannot tolerate it, with the limits of that choice explained honestly. What it should not be is a substitute presented as an equivalent. If you have been offered injections with no discussion of <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">the medical options and their side-effect profiles</a>, a conversation has been skipped, and it was the more important one.</p>

<p>You may also be offered GFC, exosomes or another branded variant. Those differ in preparation and in how much published evidence stands behind them — <a href="/blog/prp-vs-gfc-vs-exosomes">how PRP, GFC and exosomes actually differ</a> covers it. Whatever the label, the maintenance logic above applies.</p>

